i":"https://en.wikipedia.org/","lang":"en"},"internal":false,"protocol":"wiki","key":"wkJRPdjxnf"},{"type":"text","value":" (lower right; lower left is an unhatched egg).","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"eKmVt16tKp"}],"key":"lqL1HxBbxI"}],"key":"HGeyr8L0sF"}],"label":"comp-blastulae","identifier":"comp-blastulae","enumerator":"1","html_id":"comp-blastulae","key":"heuLpCUiB0"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As shown in these examples, newly-hatched embryos and other early-stage marine invertebrate larvae typically have simple roughly spheroidal external surfaces, some or all of which are covered with cilia that provide propulsion.\nThe internal morphologies are also typically quite simple, with an internal volume containing (a usually unknown) material that is sometimes accompanied by other inclusions such as lipid globules or calcium carbonate particles.","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"PLjTcqD67a"}],"key":"FUBNJWoIaC"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Despite their simple morphologies, nearly all blastulae and other early-stage larvae have a significant capacity to swim.\nThis means that, at least at some times and locations within some habitats, these simple morphologies successfully solve the problems of propulsion, orientation and stability.","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"gaXnXMc58c"}],"key":"hBPV6Ti6L4"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Their minimal but diverse morphologies, combined with their success as swimmers, raise interesting questions in biomechanics:","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"yhULPA6v2N"}],"key":"sadVTqHBXu"},{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"What are the constraints on larval size and shape resulting from the need to swim?","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"qdo5AFs5fb"}],"key":"uSa0OOGQkB"}],"key":"OvkBFvRefT"},{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"What are the constraints on swimming imposed by the need to satisfy other requirements and limitations in larval development?","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"aoyf30UHlm"}],"key":"fet9oJz95l"}],"key":"MRlqJVGkC5"},{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"How do each of these interact with environmental variations, and with the different life histories adopted by different taxa?","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"DcXhRnFuh7"}],"key":"GEpZvea4qb"}],"key":"lw7PNTpAQn"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The remainder of this Unit address these questions, with an exploration of some ways in which the requirements for successful swimming may constrain early larval development, and other aspects of marine invertebrate life history.","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"MTrtUxvYKS"}],"key":"cv8tmTFSNa"},{"type":"footnoteDefinition","identifier":"vrt","label":"vrt","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Aquatic environments often vary over much shorter distances in the vertical direction than in horizontal directions. For example, light levels often decrease over a few meters depth, but remain essentially similar for 10s or 100s of kilometers horizontally. Therefore, swimming to change exposure to environmental conditions is often much more effective in the vertical than horizontal directions.","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"key":"EwtmyZDqCn"}],"key":"Uomh7MYOqD"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"N3NA3UZZ2Q"},{"type":"footnoteDefinition","identifier":"ech","label":"ech","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Echinoids are sea urchins, which include the sand dollars.","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"yy3JUVBzhB"}],"key":"vw7kxBxAug"}],"enumerator":"2","key":"AvoePWqzNG"}],"key":"LiLCyiCblH"}],"key":"LD0fRRKad3"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"🏞️ Size and swimming in early-stage marine invertebrate larvae","url":"/chimeraswim","group":"Biomechanics"},"next":{"title":"📖️️ Allometries in early larval swimming","url":"/sizeswim","group":"Biomechanics"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3003"}